        <ARI_ChangeLog>2026-10-15T13:57:50+00:00 | migration (#229) | Moved to prevalence &amp; incidence figures: Prevalence US 14711.0/100k</ARI_ChangeLog>
        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Chronic Articular AOSD - Persistent arthritis as the dominant feature.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Intermittent AOSD - Recurring episodes with symptom-free periods.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Monophasic AOSD - A single episode that resolves completely.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Systemic AOSD - Severe inflammation with high fever, rash, and organ involvement.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_DOID>14256</ARI_DOID>
        <ARI_DXCODE>239920006</ARI_DXCODE>
        <ARI_DefSource>Mitrovic 2019; https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-19055-2_6</ARI_DefSource>
        <ARI_ICD10>M06.1</ARI_ICD10>
        <ARI_ID>ARI:0001008</ARI_ID>
        <ARI_MESH>D016706</ARI_MESH>
        <ARI_MONDO>0019355</ARI_MONDO>
        <ARI_NCI>C197832</ARI_NCI>
        <ARI_OMOP>4344166</ARI_OMOP>
        <ARI_ORPHANET>829</ARI_ORPHANET>
        <ARI_Obsolete>false</ARI_Obsolete>
        <ARI_PrevalenceDesc>Female 9271/100k; Male 5440/100k; F:M ratio 1.70423</ARI_PrevalenceDesc>
        <ARI_RefLink>Adult Onset Still’s Disease Support Group | https://www.facebook.com/groups/22056595992/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Arthritis Foundation | http://www.arthritis.org/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>International Still&apos;s Disease Foundation | http://www.stillsdisease.org/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Cleveland Clinic | https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/adult-onset-stills-disease-aosd</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Healthline | https://www.healthline.com/health/adult-stills-disease</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Living with an Invisible Disease | https://www.sobi.com/en/stories/living-invisible-disease</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Not Standing Still&apos;s Disease | https://www.notstandingstillsdisease.com/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Surviving Stills: Amy’s Story | https://versusarthritis.org/news/2020/november/surviving-still-s-amy-s-story/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_SNOMED>239920006</ARI_SNOMED>
        <ARI_SurveyCode>AOSD</ARI_SurveyCode>
        <ARI_Synonym>Adult Still&apos;s disease</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_UMLS>C0085253</ARI_UMLS>
        <ARI_Version>2</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Adult onset Still&apos;s disease (AoSD) is a rare form of inflammatory arthritis that affects the entire body, impacting joints and organs. Typically affecting individuals over the age of 16, patients often endure bouts of elevated fever, joint discomfort, and skin rash. Blood tests commonly indicate elevated white blood cell counts. Despite extensive research, the exact cause of AoSD remains elusive, with uncertainty over whether it stems from autoimmune or autoinflammatory origins. The disease&apos;s progression varies widely among patients; while some experience sudden onset followed by swift and complete recovery, others contend with chronic manifestations or symptom flare-ups alternating with periods of remission. AoSD bears a resemblance to systemic juvenile rheumatoid arthritis, differing primarily in the age of onset. Although a definitive cure remains elusive, treatment options exist to manage inflammation and mitigate potential organ damage. There are three main patterns of the clinical course of AoSD namely monophasic, intermittent, and chronic. There is an approximately equal distribution in each category however some studies have shown a chronic articular pattern to be more common.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Adult onset Still&apos;s disease</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


