        <ARI_ChangeLog>2026-07-11 04:10 | user | Edited: umls</ARI_ChangeLog>
        <ARI_ChangeLog>2026-07-11 04:34 | KrishnaTO | Cross-reference review: confirmed SNOMED 402397006; confirmed OMOP 4224623; flagged DOID 0080747; flagged MESH D000080223</ARI_ChangeLog>
        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Autoimmune Urticaria, Chronic Spontaneous - Persistent hives without an allergen.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Autoimmune Urticaria, Inducible - Triggered by external stimuli like pressure or heat.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_DXCODE>402397006</ARI_DXCODE>
        <ARI_DefSource>Zuberbier 2010; https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20456386/</ARI_DefSource>
        <ARI_ICD10>L50.8</ARI_ICD10>
        <ARI_ID>ARI:0001049</ARI_ID>
        <ARI_MONDO>0025513</ARI_MONDO>
        <ARI_OMOP>4224623</ARI_OMOP>
        <ARI_Obsolete>false</ARI_Obsolete>
        <ARI_Pubmed>https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20456386/</ARI_Pubmed>
        <ARI_RefLink>Asthma and Allergy Foundation | https://aafa.org/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Cleveland Clinic | https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/22900-chronic-hives-chronic-idiopathic-urticaria</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Healthline | https://www.healthline.com/health/skin/autoimmune-hives</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Living with the Mystery of Chronic Hives | http://livingwithhives.blogspot.com/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_SNOMED>402397006</ARI_SNOMED>
        <ARI_SurveyCode>AU</ARI_SurveyCode>
        <ARI_Synonym>Chronic idiopathic urticaria</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>Chronic urticaria</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>Physical urticaria</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_UMLS>C1304191</ARI_UMLS>
        <ARI_Version>2</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Autoimmune urticaria occurs when the immune system attacks the normal tissues of the body, resulting in hives. The condition becomes chronic when the lesions persists longer than 6 weeks. Chronic autoimmune urticaria has been found to be associated with autoimmune thyroid disease.

Urticaria can also be caused by allergic reaction to plants like poison ivy, dyes found in clothing, and cosmetics. An allergic reaction is not an autoimmune disease because there is no involvement by the adaptive immune system and auto-antibodies that attack your own cells are not involved. There are antibodies (IgE antibodies), but they target the invading molecules (e.g., poison ivy), not the cells in the skin. In autoimmune urticaria, the antibodies created are attacking the skin itself, not an invading molecule.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Autoimmune urticaria</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- https://diseases.autoimmuneregistry.org/disease/ARI_0001050 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://diseases.autoimmuneregistry.org/disease/ARI_0001050">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.aurint.org/ontologies/ari-disease#AutoimmuneDisease"/>
        <hasSymptom rdf:resource="http://www.aurint.org/ontologies/ari-disease#Sym_0001050_385"/>
        <targetsTissue rdf:resource="http://www.aurint.org/ontologies/ari-disease#Tissue_Skin"/>
        <ageRange>20-40 years</ageRange>
        <diseaseCategory>Autoimmune</diseaseCategory>
        <evidenceQuality>Antibody</evidenceQuality>
        <ARI_ChangeLog>2026-06-15 10:37 | Importer | Imported from ARI core reports</ARI_ChangeLog>
        <ARI_ChangeLog>2026-07-11 04:34 | KrishnaTO | Cross-reference review: confirmed SNOMED 402402005; confirmed OMOP 4221673</ARI_ChangeLog>
        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Autoimmune Urticaria/Angioedema, Complement-Mediated - Linked to complement system dysregulation.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Autoimmune Urticaria/Angioedema, Mast Cell-Mediated - Caused by abnormal immune response.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_DXCODE>402402005</ARI_DXCODE>
        <ARI_DefSource>Murdaca 2023; https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9953398/</ARI_DefSource>
        <ARI_ID>ARI:0001050</ARI_ID>
        <ARI_OMOP>4221673</ARI_OMOP>
        <ARI_Obsolete>false</ARI_Obsolete>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Cleveland Clinic | https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/22900-chronic-hives-chronic-idiopathic-urticaria</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Healthline | https://www.healthline.com/health/skin/autoimmune-hives</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_SNOMED>402402005</ARI_SNOMED>
        <ARI_SurveyCode>NULL</ARI_SurveyCode>
        <ARI_Version>2</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Both autoimmune urticaria and autoimmune angioedema are skin conditions associated with other autoimmune diseases. Urticaria is marked by raised, itchy, and red or pale areas, known as hives. Angioedema is swelling in skin and mucous membranes, including the mouth and throat, and is a more serious condition than urticaria. 

While they are also diseases on their own, patients will receive a diagnosis of a chronic version of the disease because the ICD coding system used in the medical record does not have codes for either disease. The SNOMED coding system (used in Europe) has a code for each condition, and for this combined term.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Autoimmune urticaria and/or angioedema</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


