        <ARI_RefLink>Iritis and Uveitis Support Group | https://www.facebook.com/pg/Iritis-and-Uveitis-Support-Group-144137868968536/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Cleveland Clinic | https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/14414-uveitis</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Healthline | https://www.healthline.com/health/uveitis</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Living with Uveitis | https://livingwithuveitis.com/blog/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>Olivia’s Vision | http://www.oliviasvision.org/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_RefLink>The Ocular Immunology and Uveitis Foundation | https://uveitis.org/</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_SNOMED>314429009</ARI_SNOMED>
        <ARI_SurveyCode>IU</ARI_SurveyCode>
        <ARI_Synonym>Chronic cyclitis</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>Cyclochorioretinitis</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>IU</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>Peripheral Uveitis</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>Peripheral uveoretinitis</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_Synonym>Vitritis</ARI_Synonym>
        <ARI_UMLS>C0042166</ARI_UMLS>
        <ARI_Version>2</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Intermediate uveitis is a disorder that causes inflammation in the vitreous humour, the gel that fills the eye. In this condition, cells from the blood may penetrate the eye&apos;s interior and be found suspended in the vitreous humour. Normally, both eyes are affected. Patients with the pars planitis subtype of intermediate uveitis also have additional inflammation in the pars plana (the layer of the eye between the iris and the vitreous humour). This occurs when cells from the vitreous humour leak into the pars plana. This subtype is more common in children, while non-pars planitis intermediate uveitis is more common in adults. The non-pars planitis subtype is especially common in those with systemic conditions, such as sarcoidosis and multiple sclerosis, or in those who have certain infections. Some cases without infection may be caused by autoimmune activity. Patients of both pars planitis and non-pars planitis subtypes often experience floaters and blurry vision. Without medical attention, vision loss and blindness may occur in severe cases; however, treatments are available to prevent this.

There are other forms of uveitis that can be autoimmune.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Intermediate uveitis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- https://diseases.autoimmuneregistry.org/disease/ARI_0001116 -->

