        <ARI_ClinicalSubtype>Rasmussen Syndrome, Unilateral - Affects one hemisphere.</ARI_ClinicalSubtype>
        <ARI_DXCODE>230191005, 435341000124104</ARI_DXCODE>
        <ARI_DefSource>Bien 2013; https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23216622/</ARI_DefSource>
        <ARI_ID>ARI:0001175</ARI_ID>
        <ARI_OMOP>4041672, 763040</ARI_OMOP>
        <ARI_Obsolete>false</ARI_Obsolete>
        <ARI_Pubmed>https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23216622/</ARI_Pubmed>
        <ARI_RefLink>Link to Cleveland Clinic | https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6092-rasmussens-encephalitis</ARI_RefLink>
        <ARI_SNOMED>230191005</ARI_SNOMED>
        <ARI_SNOMED>435341000124104</ARI_SNOMED>
        <ARI_SurveyCode>RAS</ARI_SurveyCode>
        <ARI_Version>2</ARI_Version>
        <rdfs:comment>Rasmussen syndrome, or Rasmussen’s Encephalitis (RE), is a rare, progressive disease that involves cognitive decline due to inflammation of one half of the brain. Those with Rasmussen syndrome experience frequent seizures and eventual permanent brain damage. This disease mostly affects children ages 2 and 10, but can also affect adolescents and adults. Treatments for this disease include antiseizure medication, immunotherapy, and in more severe cases, brain surgery. The cause of Rasmussen&apos;s syndrome is unknown, however, researchers believe that it may be an autoimmune condition that affects one half of the brain or may occur due to an unknown virus entering the brain.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Rasmussen syndrome</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


